age.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.stevenage.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"6.00pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://democracy.stevenage.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Stevenage's accounts unverified for four years; auditors charge \u00a3258,000 to rebuild assurance","lead":"Stevenage Council has received disclaimed audit opinions (where auditors could not confirm the accounts were true and fair) for four consecutive financial years from 2021/22 to 2024/25. This leaves its accounts unverified and opening balances carrying material uncertainty. The external auditor reports that significant transactions and management judgements from these years may not have been subject to audit at all. To rebuild assurance, auditors are charging an additional \u00a3258,471 in the current year, on top of standard audit fees of \u00a3226,032. The council does not expect a clean audit opinion until 2027/28. Auditors have flagged that success depends on the council's finance function being sufficiently resourced, and have identified specific risk factors around Queensway LLP transactions requiring investigation across all four years.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"1. SBC SIAS Progress Report - Audit Committee 16.09.2026 - issued 01.09.2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.stevenage.gov.uk/documents/s43809/43809.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Four years of unverified accounts costs Stevenage \u00a3358,000 in extra audit fees","lead":"Stevenage's financial statements have not been verified by auditors for four consecutive years, the council's Audit Committee heard on 16 September. The auditors issued disclaimed opinions for 2021/22 through 2024/25 \u2014 meaning they could not confirm whether the accounts were fairly presented \u2014 leaving material uncertainty over the accuracy of reported balances. The additional cost of rebuilding assurance is \u00a3358,173 beyond standard audit fees. A clean audit opinion is not expected until 2027/28, just weeks before the April 2028 local government reorganisation. The auditors have flagged that success depends on the council's finance function being \"sufficiently resourced\" \u2014 explicitly marking it as a requirement that must be met.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"1. SBC SIAS Progress Report - Audit Committee 16.09.2026 - issued 01.09.2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.stevenage.gov.uk/documents/s43809/43809.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Stevenage's audit bill doubles to rebuild four years of unverified accounts","lead":"The council's Audit Committee heard that Stevenage has received disclaimed audit opinions \u2014 meaning the auditor could not form an opinion on whether the accounts are correct \u2014 for four consecutive financial years from 2021/22 to 2024/25. The auditor is charging an additional \u00a3258,471 in 2025/26, on top of a standard fee of \u00a3226,032, to rebuild assurance through detailed testing of the disclaimed periods. A clean audit opinion is not expected until 2027/28, weeks before the April 2028 local government reorganisation. The auditor has flagged that success depends on the council's finance function being \"sufficiently resourced to support both the annual audit process for 2025/26 and future financial years\". Queensway LLP transactions have been separately identified as carrying specific risk factors requiring investigation across all four disclaimed years.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"1. SBC SIAS Progress Report - Audit Committee 16.09.2026 - issued 01.09.2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.stevenage.gov.uk/documents/s43809/43809.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"6. Annual Treasury Management Review Prudential Indicators 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.stevenage.gov.uk/documents/s43818/43818.pdf","headline":"Council's internal borrowing strategy is eroding: financing margins collapsed while council took precautionary borrowing to hedge rate risk","detail":"The GF financing cost ratio deteriorated sharply from minus 9% in 2024/25 to minus 2% in 2025/26, reflecting the cost of funding unfunded capital expenditure. Despite this, council took \u00a312M new PWLB borrowing at 4.31% specifically to ensure resilience against future rate increases, signalling concern that its historic strategy of avoiding external borrowing cannot be sustained.","category":"borrowing","matters":"The council has relied on investment returns exceeding borrowing costs to make internal borrowing profitable. As that margin shrinks and rates remain elevated, the council will be forced into external borrowing at higher rates, increasing future revenue costs. The hedging borrowing signals the council expects this pressure.","figures":"GF financing costs (9)% in 2024/25, (2)% in 2025/26; new borrowing \u00a312M at 4.31% for 5 years; investment returns averaged 4.35% on \u00a360M balances","quote":"The Council has taken some limited borrowing in 2025/26 (\u00a312 Million) to ensure the Council's cashflow position is resilient and to ensure that if interest rates increase, large amounts of borrowing required are not all taken at higher rates","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"6. Annual Treasury Management Review Prudential Indicators 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.stevenage.gov.uk/documents/s43818/43818.pdf","headline":"Council underspent capital by 49% (\u00a350M), with major schemes slipping into future years","detail":"Budgeted capital expenditure was \u00a3103.2M but actual was \u00a353.0M. General Fund underspent 57% (\u00a351.8M budget, \u00a322.3M actual), HRA 40% (\u00a351.4M budget, \u00a330.7M actual). Council attributes this to rephasing of schemes into future years.","category":"capital","matters":"Large-scale capital underspend raises questions about delivery capability and creates cascading effects: schemes need refinancing later (when rates could be higher), and future years face heavier spending combined with less available borrowing headroom.","figures":"Total budget \u00a3103,220K, actual \u00a353,041K, variance \u00a350,179K (49%)","quote":"There was rephasing of planned capital expenditure from 2025/26 into future years resulting in an overall reduction in the use of borrowing to finance capital expenditure","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"6. Annual Treasury Management Review Prudential Indicators 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.stevenage.gov.uk/documents/s43818/43818.pdf","headline":"High interest rates threaten the Housing Revenue Account's ability to fund its approved 30-year business plan","detail":"The council explicitly identifies that sustained high rates pose a risk to the HRA's capacity to fund its 30-year spending plans, and has not yet revised the HRA forecast. The HRA represents 80% of the council's total Capital Financing Requirement (\u00a3273.8M of \u00a3343.1M), so this is the dominant factor in the council's long-term sustainability.","category":"borrowing","matters":"The HRA is the largest capital commitment on the council's balance sheet. If the largest part of the council's capital programme cannot fund itself at current rates, that signals a fundamental sustainability issue and potential need to draw on general reserves.","figures":"HRA CFR \u00a3273,845K (80% of total \u00a3343,111K); HRA financing costs 12% of HRA net revenue stream","quote":"There is a risk to the HRA BP's ability to fund the approved 30-year spending plans if interest rates continue at the current high level","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"6. Annual Treasury Management Review Prudential Indicators 2025-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.stevenage.gov.uk/documents/s43818/43818.pdf","headline":"Council borrowed \u00a37.5 million to lend to Swingate LLP for property development as part of joint venture with Mace","detail":"Non-treasury lending to subsidiaries increased from \u00a314.1M to \u00a323.3M. Swingate (development company) is owed \u00a310.3M repayable 2027/28, and Marshgate (housing company) is owed \u00a313.0M repayable 2026/27. The \u00a37.5M Swingate borrowing represents new leverage of development risk on the council's balance sheet.","category":"capital","matters":"Lenders care deeply about off-balance-sheet development risk. Development companies can fail, leaving the council with write-offs and capital losses, especially when the council is leveraged into them.","figures":"Swingate \u00a310,262K; Marshgate \u00a312,972K; total subsidiary lending increase from \u00a314,077K to \u00a323,316K","quote":"\u00a37.5 Million relating to borrowing used to finance additional lending to Swingate LLP as part of the joint venture with Mace","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-16","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"3. Stevenage BC - Build-back Plan September 2026","paper_url":"https://democracy.stevenage.gov.uk/documents/s43828/43828.pdf","headline":"Queensway LLP transactions identified as specific risk factor requiring investigation across all four disclaimed years","detail":"The auditor's risk assessment identifies 'specific risk factors over the Queensway LLP transactions' despite otherwise rating the council at the lower end of rebuild risk. This work is marked in progress across 2021/22 through 2024/25 with scheduled completion in 2025/26.","category":"capital","matters":"A named transaction area flagged as a specific risk exception across an entire disclaimed period suggests either control weaknesses, accounting uncertainty, or complexity that could materially affect the council's balance sheet or capital position.","figures":"None","quote":"with specific risk factors over the Queensway LLP transactions","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-16"},"name":"Stevenage","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-25","since_iso":"2026-08-26","distress":false,"headline":"Council's internal borrowing strategy is eroding: financing margins collapsed while council took precautionary borrowing to hedge rate risk","lead":"The council has relied on investment returns exceeding borrowing costs to make internal borrowing profitable. As that margin shrinks and rates remain elevated, the council will be forced into external borrowing at higher rates, increasing future revenue\u2026","also":["60 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-10-08","n_findings":61,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"6. Annual Treasury Management Review Prudential Indicators 2025-26","top_paper_url":"https://democracy.stevenage.gov.uk/documents/s43818/43818.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":143,"news_drafts_total":3}}